.he "network (4)                  4/6/83                   network (4)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
DEVICE
.in +5
.br
.bd
network
- the Micronix network
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Micronix allows any number of Decisions to be interconnected in any
pattern with simple, low cost hardware.  Files can be copied from any
source to any destination on the network (see cp in section 1), and
any user may send mail to any other user (see mail in section 1).
This is all done with ordinary serial I/O ports and 3-conductor
unshielded cables.  Error checking is extensive, so that cables may be
cut and patched, and machines may be crashed and restarted, all with
good assurance that all files will arrive intact.  There is a penalty
for this generality and low cost, however.  The transfer rate is slow
(about 40-50 bytes per second).  But since all network activity is in
the background, users need not wait idly for file transfers to
finish.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
Two-machine network
.in +5
.br
Let's begin by describing the simplest network:  two machines with one
cable between them.  First, you must choose names for the two
machines.  These names are arbitrary; the only requirement is that
each machine on the network must have a unique name.  The names should
also be short and easy to type.  In this case, let's call them "sales"
and "doc".

Next, you must buy or build a cable long enough to connect them.  (See
cables in section 4.)  Then you must choose a free serial port on each
machine (see ports in section 4).  Let's say that you have chosen
ttyD on doc, and ttyF on sales.  The cable can be plugged in at this
point -- either end to either machine.

Now you must edit three files on each machine.  These files are:

.nf
.in +5
/etc/ttys
/etc/netmap
/etc/rc
.in -5
.fi

The /etc/ttys file tells which IO ports on the local machine are
connected to the network.  The /etc/netmap file gives the layout of
the network.  The /etc/rc file contains commands that are run every
time the system goes multi-user.  In this case, we will insert a
command to start the "network daemon" (the background process that
runs the network).

Micronix comes with WordStar (a screen editor) and edit (a line
editor).  You may use either to edit these files.  Instructions for
using edit on the ttys file are contained in the ttys document in
section 5.

Here is what you must do to each file on each machine:

.nf
.in 0
machine   file          action

doc       /etc/ttys     Find the line that begins with ttyD.
                        Change it to read as follows:
                        ttyD 1200 net-sales

doc       /etc/netmap   Change (or create) this file to read:
                        doc
                        sales

doc       /etc/rc       Add the following lines:
                        echo Starting network
                        netdaemon

sales     /etc/ttys     Find the line that begins with ttyF.
                        Change it to read as follows:
                        ttyF 1200 net-doc

sales     /etc/netmap   Change (or create) this file to read:
                        sales
                        doc

sales     /etc/rc       Add the following lines:
                        echo Starting network
                        netdaemon
.in 5
.fi

You will have to bring the system down (with the down command) and
then start it up again before these changes take effect.  You can test
the network by typing the following command from the doc machine:

.in +5
.bd
% cp /etc/ttys sales%/tmp
.in -5

The /etc/ttys file should appear on the sales machine in the /tmp
directory.  See cp and mail in section 1 for more information on using
the network.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
Three-machine network
.in +5
.br
Now suppose we want to connect a third machine named "admin" to the
network.

The new machine can be connected to any of the existing machines.  It
is only necessary to connect it to one of them, but connecting to
more than one might speed up the network by providing a more direct
path between machines.  For example, you could connect the new machine
as follows:

.nf
.in +10
sales -------- doc
     \        /
      \      /
       admin
.in -10
.fi

This requires a total of 6 IO ports, 2 on each machine.  But messages
will be sent directly between any pair of machines.  Or you could
connect this way:

.nf
.in +5
sales -------- doc -------- admin
.in -5
.fi

This network uses only 4 ports:  1 on sales, 2 on doc, and 1 on admin.
The disadvantage is that messages between sales and admin have to go
through doc.  This will be done automatically, but it takes longer.
Since the emphasis in the design of this network is low cost, the 4
port alternative is recommended.  (But if you have no other use for
the ports, go ahead and use all 6.)

Let's assume that you are going to use the straight-line connection
(the 4 port alternative).  First, you must choose a free serial port
on doc, say ttyE, and one on admin, say ttyC.  Connect these ports
with a network cable, and change the configuration files as follows:

.nf
.in 0
         /etc/netmap     /etc/rc      /etc/ttys
         -----------     -------      ---------
sales    sales           no change    no change
         doc admin

doc      doc             no change    ttyE 1200 net-admin
         admin sales

admin    admin           netdaemon    ttyC 1200 net-doc
         doc sales
.in 5
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
General networks
.in +5
.br
The only restriction on the shape of the network is a physical one:  a
seperate serial port is required at each end of each cable.
Otherwise, the network can be a straight line, a star, a ring, or any
pattern.  The rules to follow in setting up the 3 configuration files
are as follows:

/etc/ttys -- Find the lines corresponding to each network port on the
local machine.  Put the word 1200 on each line, and in addition, put
the word net-name, where "name" is the name of the machine to which
that port is connected.

/etc/rc -- Put the command netdaemon in the file.  It should be alone
on a line.  It should appear only once, no matter how many network
ports there are on the machine.  Optionally, it can be preceeded by an
anouncement to remind the operator the network is being started.

/etc/netmap -- The name of the local machine should appear alone on
the first line.  Each succeeding line should begin with the name of a
machine that is directly connected to the local machine.  If there are
additional machines on the network, their names should appear (in any
order) following the name of the direct connection through which they
can be reached.  If there are several ways to reach an "indirect"
machine, only one connection should be given:  the one that leads to
the machine via the shortest route.

For example, let's look at several different networks, and a possible
netmap file for the "doc" machine in each network.

.nf
.in 0
Line    sales -- doc -- admin -- support    doc
                                            sales
                                            admin support


Ring         doc ------ sales               doc
              |           |                 sales
              |           |                 admin support
            admin ---- support


Star    sales ---- doc ---- admin           doc
                    |                       sales
                    |                       admin
                 support                    support


Star     doc ---- sales --- admin           doc
                    |                       sales admin support
                    |
                 support


Dipper   doc      admin --- support         doc
           \      /                         sales
            \    /                          admin support
            sales
.in 5
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
PROTOCOL
.in +5
.br
This section describes the protocol as the recovered 1.6 programs
implement it: netcp, netdaemon, netact, mail and netmail.  The
decompilation is in the source tree, under cmd/netdaemon and
cmd/netcp; the line formats below were also captured from the
distributed binaries running under the simulator.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
The line
.in +5
.br
A link is one asynchronous serial line, 1200 baud, one conversation at
a time.  Everything is sent in lines.  A line is three printable
characters of checksum, the text of the line, and a newline.  The
checksum is a 16-bit sum that starts at zero; for each character of
the text, including the newline but not the three checksum
characters,
.sp
.nf
.in +5
sum = sum + character
sum = (sum << 1) | (sum >> 15)
.in -5
.fi
.sp
The sum is sent as three six-bit groups, lowest group first, each
group plus 0x20:
.sp
.nf
.in +5
c0 = (sum & 0x3f) + 0x20
c1 = ((sum >> 6) & 0x3f) + 0x20
c2 = ((sum >> 12) & 0x3f) + 0x20
.in -5
.fi
.sp
Every character on the wire is therefore in the range 0x20 to 0x5f.
A receiver recomputes the sum over the text and compares all three
characters; any difference is an error.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
The six-bit code
.in +5
.br
Data bytes, sequence numbers and lengths are sent six bits at a time:
.sp
.nf
.in +5
character = (value & 0x3f) + 0x20
.in -5
.fi
.sp
The values of a group are packed least significant bit first.  Three
bytes of data become four characters: bits 0-5, 6-11, 12-17, and
18-23.  A group of one or two bytes at the end of a record is padded
with zero bits.  A 45 byte record is therefore 60 characters.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
The records
.in +5
.br
The lines of the protocol are:
.sp
.nf
.in +5
P n	proposal; n is the proposal number
A n	acceptance of proposal n
F	file start
Y	line acknowledged
N	line not acknowledged
S ...	a file is offered
R ...	a file is requested
M ...	a message is offered
D s l d	a data record, sequence s, l bytes, d encoded
E n	end of record
.in -5
.fi
.sp
The proposal number n is one six-bit character.  It is a counter in
the sending daemon, incremented for each proposal and wrapped at 64.
The acceptance carries the same number: the receiver changes the P of
the proposal to A and recomputes the checksum.
.sp
The S record offers a file.  Its fields are separated by single
blanks:
.sp
.nf
.in +5
S rhost rpath lhost lpath owner mode
.in -5
.fi
.sp
rhost is the machine the file is going to, rpath the name it will have
there; lhost is the machine the file comes from, lpath the name it has
there; owner is the login name from /etc/passwd; mode is the octal
permission bits.
.sp
The R record asks a machine for a file and carries no data:
.sp
.nf
.in +5
R rhost rpath lhost lpath owner
.in -5
.fi
.sp
rhost and rpath name the file to be fetched, lhost and lpath name the
copy to be made, and owner is the requester.
.sp
The M record is a message:
.sp
.nf
.in +5
M rhost ruser lhost luser
.in -5
.fi
.sp
rhost is the machine the message is going to and ruser the user there;
lhost is the sending machine and luser the user sending it.
.sp
A D record carries one piece of data.  The character after D is the
sequence number, one six-bit character; the next is the length, the
number of data bytes, one six-bit character; the rest is the data in
the six-bit code.  A data record holds at most 45 bytes, which is 60
characters.
.sp
The header line is record zero, and the sequence number of every
following line is in the character after the record letter.  For the
header this character is the blank that separates the letter from the
first field, which is the six-bit code of zero.  Data records are
numbered from one.  The E record carries the number that the next data
record would have.  A file of three data records is numbered
.sp
.nf
.in +5
S ...	0	the character after S is a blank
D ! ...	1
D " ...	2
D # ...	3
E $	4
.in -5
.fi
.sp
The sequence numbers are six bits and wrap at 64.  A receiver uses
them to notice a lost, repeated or misplaced record.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
The exchange
.in +5
.br
The daemon that has a spool entry to send proposes.  The daemon that
is idle waits for a proposal.  A transfer runs:
.sp
.nf
.in +5
sender                  receiver
P !                     A !	(accept; the P becomes an A)
F                       (waited for, not answered)
S ...                   Y
D ! ...                 Y
D " ...                 Y
...
E n                     Y
.in -5
.fi
.sp
The proposer waits for an A with its own number.  An A with another
number is ignored.  A received P instead of an A means both ends
proposed at once; this is a collision (see ERROR RECOVERY).  After the
A, the sender sends F and then the records of the spool file one at a
time; each is acknowledged with Y before the next is sent.  A N
instead of a Y causes the same line to be sent again.
.sp
The spool file holds the header, D and E lines as text, without the
checksum characters.  The daemon adds the checksum when it sends a
line, and the receiver removes it before writing the line to its own
spool file.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ERROR RECOVERY
.in +5
.br
Every read has a twenty second timeout.  The receiver answers a line
whose checksum does not verify, or a read that times out, with N; the
sender sends the same line again.  The sender makes ten attempts per
line, each with its own twenty second timeout, before it gives up on
the transfer.  On a read timeout the receiving side also sends a bare
newline, which is not a protocol line.
.sp
The receiver acknowledges a line as soon as its checksum verifies,
before it looks at the sequence number.  A line whose number is not
the expected one is reported as a sequence error and is not stored; it
does not advance the receiver's count.  The E record still ends the
transfer, so the sequence check reports a fault but does not repair
one.  A line lost on the wire is repaired by the timeout and the N,
not by numbering.
.sp
A proposal that gets no A is abandoned after the twenty second read
timeout.  The spool entry is kept and is proposed again on a later
pass.  Likewise, after the A, if F or a record cannot be delivered,
the entry stays in the spool directory and the whole transfer is tried
again later; the receiver removes the partial file it made.
.sp
When a proposal arrives while this end is waiting for its own A, this
end records a collision, sleeps for a pseudo-random interval of up to
twenty seconds, and gives up this attempt without consuming the spool
entry.  The proposal it received is left unanswered, so the other end
times out and tries again later as well.  When both ends are idle
neither sends a protocol line; a transfer starts only when one has an
entry to send.
.sp
The receiver names its incoming file after the machine at the other
end of the port, so a receiver that restarts leaves a partial file
behind.  The daemon does not sweep
the incoming directory at startup; stale files there are not
delivered.  A sender that crashes after the receiver has taken the
file but before the sender removes its spool entry will offer the file
again, and the file will be delivered twice: the protocol has no
transaction identifier and no duplicate suppression.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NAMING
.in +5
.br
Each machine has a name, one short word.  The first line of /etc/netmap
is the local machine's name.  Every later line begins with the name of
a machine directly connected by a cable; any further names on the line
are machines reachable through that connection.  The first word of the
line is the route, the next hop, for every name on the line.
.sp
A port is named in /etc/ttys by a token net-name, where name is the
machine at the other end of the cable, for example
.sp
.nf
.in +5
ttyD 1200 net-sales
.in -5
.fi
.sp
The daemon opens /dev/ttyD and treats the name sales as the identity of
that port.  The 1200 is the line speed.
.sp
A network file name is host%path, and a network mail address is
host%user; host is one or more lower case letters and is looked up in
/etc/netmap to find the route.  A name without a percent sign is local.
.sp
The spool directories are /usr/spool/net/out for work to be sent and
/usr/spool/net/in for work being received.  Every file in them is named
.sp
.nf
.in +5
base % c0 c1 c2
.in -5
.fi
.sp
where base is the route for an outbound entry, or the name of the
machine at the other end of the port for an inbound one.  The three
characters are six-bit codes of values that make the name unique:
the process id, the time, and a counter that the program increments
for each name it makes.  A slash produced by a code is changed to the
character 0 so that the name cannot leave the directory.  For example
.sp
.nf
.in +5
/usr/spool/net/out/sales%"S!
.in -5
.fi
.sp
is an entry for the machine sales.
.sp
An entry is not written in place.  netcp and netmail create a file
whose base is TEMP, write the whole record, and then make the real
name with a link and remove the temporary name.  The daemon scans for
names beginning with its own route, so a half written TEMP file is
never sent.  A relayed record is moved from the incoming to the
outgoing directory the same way, by link and unlink, under the name of
the next hop.
.sp
An R record travels to the machine that holds the file.  That machine
runs netcp, which sends an S record back to the requester, and the
requester makes the copy.  For this to work every machine's /etc/netmap
must name the route back to the machines that will answer it; a
machine with only its own name in netmap can receive but cannot answer.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
CONCURRENCY
.in +5
.br
The daemon reads /etc/ttys at startup and forks one process for each
network port.  The parent goes on to the next line of the file and
exits at the end; each child owns one port and runs the daemon loop:
.sp
.nf
.in +5
for (;;) {
	scan_in();	accept a transfer if one is proposed
	scan_out();	send one spool entry if there is one
}
.in -5
.fi
.sp
scan_in waits in accept for up to twenty seconds, so a port that has
nothing to do wakes at least that often and looks at the outgoing
spool.  There is no select and no interrupt: the daemon is a poller.
.sp
All the port processes share the two spool directories.  An outgoing
entry is named with its route, and each port process only takes
entries whose name begins with the machine at the other end of its
port, so an entry is offered on the one port that leads toward its
destination.  There is
no lock on the spool directories.  Two entries from two users are two
files and are sent one after the other.  If the same machine is
connected twice, or a netmap is written with two routes to the same
name, one entry can be found by both processes and sent twice.
.sp
One transfer at a time runs on a link.  Two ends that propose together
collide; the random backoff keeps them from colliding again in step.
While a transfer is in progress the receiving port process is busy
with it and cannot start an outgoing transfer on that port; the
sending process likewise.  Work for other ports is unaffected.
.sp
At the destination the incoming file is handed to netact, which is
started by fork and waited for.  The port process does nothing else
until the local copy, fetch or mail delivery finishes, so a slow
destination device delays that port but not the others.  The incoming
file is removed after netact returns.  netact is run only for a record
addressed to this machine; one addressed elsewhere is moved straight
into the outgoing spool, and the port process for the next hop then
sends it onward.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
cp (1), mail (1), cables (4), ports (4)
